The Multiplanetary Peace Transition: 

A Formal Theory of Strategic Interdependence in Distributed Earth-Orbit-Moon-Mars Infrastructure

ouadi Maakoul 

AI: chatGpt, Claude, Deepseek, Gemini 

Abstract

This thesis develops a rigorous game-theoretic framework for analyzing whether the transition toward a multiplanetary civilization transforms the strategic structure of international interaction. The central contribution is a formal characterization of the conditions under which strategic interdependence across distributed space infrastructure stabilizes cooperative equilibria—or, alternatively, enables coercive leverage and preemptive conflict. We construct a unified payoff function integrating dependency structure, directional asymmetry, network redundancy, offense-defense balance, and information delay. The analysis proceeds through three formal layers: (1) a static two-player game establishing equilibrium existence conditions, (2) a repeated-game formulation with imperfect public monitoring characterizing dynamic sustainability, and (3) an evolutionary analysis identifying critical mass thresholds and bistability regimes. We derive explicit propositions with complete proofs, establishing non-monotonic relationships between interdependence and cooperation stability, threshold effects for asymmetry and information delay, and the existence of a well-defined cooperative phase boundary. The framework demonstrates that multiplanetary infrastructure does not inherently produce peace; rather, it constitutes a new strategic topology whose stability properties depend on the precise architecture of dependency, redundancy, asymmetry, offense dominance, and verification capacity.

