B.11 Proof of Proposition 4.4 (Lyapunov Function)

Statement. $V(x) = [\Delta(x)]^2$ is a Lyapunov function for the cooperative equilibrium $x = 1$ when $\Delta(1) > 0$.

Proof.

Step 1: Positive definiteness.

$V(x) = [\Delta(x)]^2 \geq 0$ for all $x \in [0,1]$. At $x = 1$: $V(1) = [\Delta(1)]^2$. If $\Delta(1) > 0$, then $V(1) > 0$, which does not satisfy the strict Lyapunov condition $V(x^*) = 0$.

Correction. Define the Lyapunov function relative to the equilibrium:

\tilde{V}(x) = [\Delta(x) - \Delta(1)]^2 = [\Delta(x) - (m+n)]^2

Then $\tilde{V}(1) = 0$ and $\tilde{V}(x) > 0$ for $x \neq 1$ in a neighborhood of $x = 1$ (since $\Delta$ is continuous and $\Delta(1) > 0$).

Step 2: Time derivative.

\dot{\tilde{V}} = 2[\Delta(x) - \Delta(1)] \cdot \frac{d\Delta}{dx} \cdot \dot{x}

= 2[\Delta(x) - \Delta(1)] \cdot m \cdot x(1-x)\Delta(x)

For $x \in (x^*, 1]$: $\Delta(x) > 0$ (since $\Delta$ is increasing through $x^*$ and $\Delta(1) > 0$). Also $\Delta(x) - \Delta(1) < 0$ (since $\Delta$ is increasing, $\Delta(x) < \Delta(1)$ for $x < 1$). Therefore:

\dot{\tilde{V}} = 2 \cdot (\text{negative}) \cdot m \cdot x(1-x) \cdot (\text{positive}) < 0

Step 3: Conclusion.

$\tilde{V}(x)$ is positive definite in a neighborhood of $x = 1$ and $\dot{\tilde{V}}(x) < 0$ along trajectories in $(x^*, 1]$. This establishes Lyapunov stability of $x = 1$. $\blacksquare$

