Appendix C: Special Cases and Limiting Behavior

C.1 Zero Redundancy ($R = 0$)

When $R = 0$, the full disruption cost is realized:

D_i(a_i, a_j) = e_{ij}\mathcal{K}_i(1-R) = e_{ij}\mathcal{K}_i

The cooperation function simplifies to:

F(\theta) = \lambda b - \alpha + \lambda v(1-s)\mathcal{K}\sigma

C.2 Full Redundancy ($R = 1$)

When $R = 1$, disruption costs vanish:

D_i(a_i, a_j) = e_{ij}\mathcal{K}_i(1-1) = 0

The cooperation function becomes:

F(\theta) = \lambda b - \alpha

Cooperation is sustainable if and only if $\lambda b \geq \alpha$, independent of $\gamma$ and $\tau$. The game reduces to a standard Prisoner's Dilemma with transformed payoffs.

C.3 Zero Information Delay ($\tau = 0$)

When $\tau = 0$:

q(0) = q_0

\mathcal{K}(\gamma, 0) = \kappa_0 \gamma^\eta

The verification probability is at its maximum. The cooperation function:

F = \lambda[b + v(1-s)\kappa_0\gamma^\eta(1-R)\sigma_0 q_0 \bar{g}] - \alpha

This represents the best-case scenario for cooperation sustainability (relative to positive delay).

C.4 Infinite Information Delay ($\tau \to \infty$)

As $\tau \to \infty$:

q(\tau) \to 0

\sigma(\tau) \to 0

\mathcal{K}(\gamma,\tau) \to \infty \text{ (linear growth)}

The product:

\mathcal{K}\sigma = \kappa_0\gamma^\eta\sigma_0q_0\bar{g}(1+\mu\tau)e^{-\tau/\tau_0} \to 0

The cooperation function:

F \to \lambda b - \alpha

Cooperation depends solely on whether the cooperation surplus exceeds the temptation.

C.5 Zero Asymmetry ($A = 0$)

When $\lambda_{12} = \lambda_{21} = \lambda$:

g(\lambda, \lambda) = \exp(-\theta\lambda(1-\lambda))

This is symmetric in $\lambda$ and achieves its minimum at $\lambda = 1/2$ (where $\lambda(1-\lambda) = 1/4$ is maximized). The retaliation exposure:

\sigma = \sigma_0 q(\tau) \exp(-\theta/4)

is minimized at $\lambda = 1/2$, meaning the weaponized interdependence effect is strongest (paradoxically) at moderate symmetric dependence.

C.6 Complete Asymmetry ($A \to 2\bar{\lambda}$)

As $\lambda_{12} \to 2\bar{\lambda}$ and $\lambda_{21} \to 0$:

For Actor 2's retaliation exposure:

g(\lambda_{21}, \lambda_{12}) = \exp(-\theta \lambda_{21}(1-\lambda_{12}))

If $\lambda_{21} \to 0$ and $\lambda_{12} \to 2\bar{\lambda}$ with $2\bar{\lambda} < 1$: $g \to 1$ (no weaponized interdependence).

If $\lambda_{21} \to 0$ and $\lambda_{12} \to 1$ simultaneously: the limit depends on the product $\lambda_{21}(1-\lambda_{12})$. If this product tends to a positive constant, $g \to e^{-\theta c} < 1$. If it tends to 0, $g \to 1$. If it tends to $\infty$, $g \to 0$.

