Appendix D: Eigenvalue Analysis of the Replicator Dynamics

D.1 Linearization Matrix

The replicator dynamics are one-dimensional:

\dot{x} = f(x) = x(1-x)\Delta(x)

The linearization at any fixed point $x^*$ is the scalar:

f'(x^*) = (1-2x^*)\Delta(x^*) + x^*(1-x^*)\Delta'(x^*)

D.2 Eigenvalues at Fixed Points

At $x = 0$:

f'(0) = (1-0)\Delta(0) + 0 = \Delta(0) = n = -\delta - e\mathcal{K}(1-R) < 0

The eigenvalue is negative, confirming asymptotic stability.

At $x = 1$:

f'(1) = (1-2)\Delta(1) + 1 \cdot 0 \cdot \Delta'(1) = -\Delta(1) = -(m+n) = -F(\theta)

The eigenvalue is negative if $F(\theta) > 0$ (cooperation is Nash), confirming asymptotic stability in this case.

At $x = x^*$ (if it exists):

f'(x^*) = (1-2x^*)\Delta(x^*) + x^*(1-x^*)\Delta'(x^*)

= 0 + x^*(1-x^*)m = x^*(1-x^*)m > 0

The eigenvalue is positive (since $m > 0$ for interior fixed point existence), confirming instability.

D.3 Bifurcation Analysis

The system undergoes a bifurcation when $F(\theta) = 0$ (i.e., $m + n = 0$). At this point:

· The interior fixed point $x^* = -n/m$ approaches $x = 1$ (since $m \to -n$)
· The eigenvalue at $x = 1$ approaches 0
· The system transitions from bistability (for $F > 0$) to monostability at $x = 0$ (for $F < 0$)

This is a transcritical bifurcation at $F = 0$.

Proposition D.1 (Bifurcation). The replicator dynamics undergo a transcritical bifurcation at $F(\theta) = 0$, where the interior fixed point $x^*$ collides with the boundary fixed point $x = 1$ and exchanges stability.

Proof. At $F = 0$: $m + n = 0$, so $m = -n$. The interior fixed point:

x^* = -\frac{n}{m} = -\frac{n}{-n} = 1

The interior fixed point coincides with the boundary fixed point. For $F < 0$: $m + n < 0$, so $m < -n$, and $x^* = -n/m > 1$ (outside the domain). For $F > 0$: $m + n > 0$, so $m > -n$, and $x^* = -n/m < 1$ (inside the domain). This confirms the transcritical bifurcation structure. $\blacksquare$

