Appendix G: Generalization to n Actors

G.1 Network Structure

Consider $n$ actors with dependency matrix $\Lambda \in [0,1]^{n \times n}$, $\lambda_{ii} = 0$. The payoff to actor $i$:

U_i(a_i, \mathbf{a}_{-i}) = \Pi_i^{\text{Local}}(a_i) + \sum_{j \neq i} \lambda_{ij}b_{ij}\mathbf{1}[a_i = a_j = C] - \sum_{j \neq i} e_{ij}\mathcal{K}_i(1-R)\mathbf{1}[a_i = C, a_j = D]

G.2 All-Cooperate Nash Equilibrium

Proposition G.1. The profile $(C, C, \ldots, C)$ is a Nash equilibrium if and only if for all $i$:

\sum_{j \neq i} \lambda_{ij}b_{ij} + \sum_{j \neq i} e_{ji}\mathcal{K}_j(1-R)\sigma_i \geq \alpha_i

where $\sigma_i = \sigma_{i,0}q(\tau)\prod_{j \neq i} g(\lambda_{ji}, \lambda_{ij})$ (the retaliation exposure depends on all dyadic relationships).

Proof. The deviation payoff for actor $i$ (defecting while all others cooperate) is:

U_i(D, \mathbf{C}_{-i}) = \beta + \alpha_i - \sum_{j \neq i} e_{ji}\mathcal{K}_j(1-R)\sigma_i

The cooperation payoff is:

U_i(C, \mathbf{C}_{-i}) = \beta + \sum_{j \neq i} \lambda_{ij}b_{ij}

Setting $U_i(C, \mathbf{C}_{-i}) \geq U_i(D, \mathbf{C}_{-i})$ yields the condition. $\blacksquare$

G.3 Coalition Formation

The n-actor extension allows for coalition formation. A coalition $S \subset \{1, \ldots, n\}$ can sustain cooperation among its members while defecting against outsiders. The stability of such partial cooperation depends on the network structure and the distribution of dependencies.

G.4 Free-Rider Problem

If the cooperation surplus $b_{ij}$ is non-excludable (public good), then actors may free-ride on the cooperation of others. This reduces the incentive to cooperate and shrinks the cooperative region. The formal analysis requires introducing a public goods component to the payoff function.

