Appendix H: Summary of Theorems with Complete Statements

Theorem H.1 (Interdependence Non-Monotonicity)

Under the model assumptions with weaponized interdependence ($\theta > 0$), the relationship between aggregate interdependence $\bar{\lambda}$ and cooperation stability is non-monotonic: cooperation is unsustainable for very low $\bar{\lambda}$ (insufficient surplus), potentially sustainable for intermediate $\bar{\lambda}$, and potentially unsustainable for very high $\bar{\lambda}$ with asymmetry (retaliation credibility collapse).

Theorem H.2 (Offense-Dominance Duality)

Offense dominance ($\gamma > 1$) has dual effects: deterrence (stabilizing) and preemption (destabilizing). The net effect depends on the prior probability of defection $p$ and first-mover advantage $\phi$. Cooperation is sustainable for intermediate $\gamma$ and fails for extreme $\gamma$ when $p > 0$ or $\phi > 0$.

Theorem H.3 (Verification-Redundancy Substitution)

Network redundancy $R$ and verification capacity $q(\tau)$ are strategic substitutes. The cross-partial derivative of the cooperation sustainability condition with respect to $R$ and $q$ is positive, indicating that increasing $R$ reduces the sensitivity of cooperation to changes in $q$.

Theorem H.4 (Critical Mass for Cooperation)

In the evolutionary formulation, cooperation requires an initial critical mass $x^*$ of cooperators. The threshold depends on parameters, with higher cooperation surplus and retaliation credibility lowering the threshold, and higher redundancy and temptation raising it.

Theorem H.5 (Weaponized Interdependence Threshold)

There exists a critical asymmetry $A^*$ such that for $A > A^*$, $(C,C)$ is not a Nash equilibrium, regardless of aggregate interdependence. The threshold depends on the sensitivity parameter $\theta$ and other model parameters.

