Appendix N: Formal Statement of Research Hypotheses

Hypothesis H1 (Interdependence Threshold)

Null: There exists $\lambda^* > 0$ such that for all $\lambda > \lambda^*$, $(C,C)$ is a Nash equilibrium.

Alternative: No finite $\lambda^*$ exists (cooperation is never sustainable).

Test: The existence of $\lambda^*$ follows from Proposition 2.2 when $b + v(1-s)\mathcal{K}(1-R)\sigma > 0$. The null is rejected if this condition fails.

Hypothesis H2 (Asymmetry Destabilization)

Null: There exists $A^* < \infty$ such that for all $A > A^*$, $(C,C)$ is not a Nash equilibrium.

Alternative: $A^* = \infty$ (asymmetry never matters).

Test: The existence of finite $A^*$ follows from Proposition 2.4 when $\theta$ is sufficiently large. The null is rejected if $\sigma$ is independent of asymmetry ($\theta = 0$).

Hypothesis H3 (Information Delay Destabilization)

Null: There exists $\tau^* < \infty$ such that for all $\tau > \tau^*$, $(C,C)$ is not a Nash equilibrium.

Alternative: $\tau^* = \infty$ (delay never matters).

Test: The existence of finite $\tau^*$ follows from Proposition 2.6 when $\lambda b < \alpha$. The null is rejected if $\lambda b \geq \alpha$ (cooperation surplus alone is sufficient).

Hypothesis H4 (Redundancy Moderation)

Null: The critical offense-defense threshold $\gamma^*$ is increasing in $R$.

Alternative: $\gamma^*$ is independent of $R$.

Test: From the cooperation condition, $\gamma^*$ depends on $(1-R)$ multiplicatively. The null holds.

Hypothesis H5 (Weaponized Interdependence)

Null: There exist parameter values such that increasing $\lambda_{12}$ (with $\lambda_{21}$ fixed) moves the system from $\Theta_C$ to outside $\Theta_C$.

Alternative: Increasing $\lambda_{12}$ never destabilizes cooperation.

Test: The null holds for sufficiently large $\theta$ (as demonstrated in Proposition 5.3). The alternative holds for $\theta = 0$.

