Appendix O: Limitations and Boundary Conditions

O.1 Two-Actor Assumption

The formal analysis is restricted to two actors. The n-actor extension (Appendix G) provides the framework but does not fully characterize equilibrium behavior. Key limitations:

· Coalition formation is not modeled
· Free-riding is not formalized
· Sanctioning mechanisms for n > 2 are not analyzed

O.2 Symmetric Simplifications

Many propositions assume symmetry for tractability. The asymmetric case (Propositions 2.3, 2.4) provides partial results but does not fully characterize the asymmetric phase diagram.

O.3 Static vs. Dynamic Analysis

The static Nash equilibrium analysis (Section 2) identifies existence conditions but does not address reachability or transition dynamics. The repeated game (Section 3) addresses sustainability but assumes trigger strategies. The evolutionary analysis (Section 4) addresses population dynamics but assumes a well-mixed population.

O.4 Parameter Calibration

The model contains numerous parameters ($\beta, \alpha, \delta, b, v, s, \kappa_0, \eta, \mu, \sigma_0, q_0, \tau_0, \theta, \phi, \delta$) that require empirical calibration. The theoretical results identify qualitative relationships, but quantitative predictions require parameter estimation from engineering and astropolitical analysis.

O.5 Information Structure

The model assumes common knowledge of the game structure and payoffs, with imperfect monitoring of actions. It does not model:

· Strategic information revelation
· Signaling
· Reputation building beyond simple trigger strategies
· Learning about the other actor's type

O.6 Time Structure

The repeated game assumes discrete time with synchronized actions. Real-world interactions may involve:

· Continuous time
· Asynchronous decisions
· Variable delays between action and observation
· Multiple simultaneous interactions across different infrastructure layers

